1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:04,940
So at this stage, we have discussed almost every code block that is available to us.

2
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:10,040
However, there's just one more thing a code block and a coding concept so important to programming

3
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:15,890
that I needed to wait until you were a pretty competent programmer before I introduced it to you.

4
00:00:15,890 --> 00:00:18,710
And that is functions.

5
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:22,130
So what is a function and why should we care about it?

6
00:00:23,030 --> 00:00:27,290
Imagine you have a toy making machine, which would be pretty cool.

7
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:32,000
So you put in some materials, press a button, and out comes a toy car.

8
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:37,340
And every time you want a new car, you just press the button again and out comes a car.

9
00:00:37,340 --> 00:00:41,360
Although the only problem with this machine is each time it's the same car.

10
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:47,660
Now imagine you have another machine that is even better, because if you want a red car, you put red

11
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:49,250
paint and out comes a red car.

12
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:52,160
Or if you want a blue car, you put in blue paint and out comes a blue car.

13
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:53,180
You get the idea.

14
00:00:53,510 --> 00:00:59,270
Well, both of these machines are examples of functions, except one of the functions allows inputs

15
00:00:59,270 --> 00:01:01,100
which can change the output.

16
00:01:01,910 --> 00:01:07,130
Now, in the world of coding, functions are like that magical button.

17
00:01:07,130 --> 00:01:13,880
You write a piece of code once, and then you can use it over and over again just by calling the functions

18
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:14,570
name.

19
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:20,390
Now some functions can accept inputs like a number or string, and depending on what the input is,

20
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:22,640
determines what output the function will give you.

21
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:29,030
Just like our car making machine that gives different color cars, but as well some functions will always

22
00:01:29,030 --> 00:01:31,280
return the same output.

23
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:33,170
So how do functions work?

24
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:36,260
Well first we write a function.

25
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:40,880
It's like building our toy making machine, setting it up to make a particular toy.

26
00:01:41,660 --> 00:01:47,360
Next, any time we need that specific task done, we call the function just like pressing the button

27
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:48,200
on the machine.

28
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:53,000
The function then executes the task and gives us the result, just like the machine popping out a toy

29
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:53,450
car.

30
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:57,220
So why are functions important in programming?

31
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:00,040
I'm sure you could probably already guess why.

32
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:06,520
Firstly, saving time without functions, we would have to write the same code again and again and again

33
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:08,350
for the same similar tasks.

34
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:09,880
Functions save us time.

35
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:13,330
We write once and we use many, many times.

36
00:02:13,850 --> 00:02:14,720
Next.

37
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:19,460
Organizing code functions help us keep our code clean and organized.

38
00:02:19,490 --> 00:02:24,170
Each function has its job, and it makes it easier to track down and fix any issues.

39
00:02:24,890 --> 00:02:27,110
Thirdly, less mistakes.

40
00:02:27,470 --> 00:02:31,820
Having one piece of code means there's only one place to check and fix if something goes wrong.

41
00:02:31,850 --> 00:02:34,100
No more searching through blocks and blocks of code.

42
00:02:34,310 --> 00:02:40,790
And lastly, sharing code functions can also be shared between different parts of our program or even

43
00:02:40,790 --> 00:02:42,230
between different programs.

44
00:02:42,230 --> 00:02:45,350
And as you know, reusable code is so important.

45
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:46,640
So imagine this.

46
00:02:46,970 --> 00:02:51,140
You're creating an exciting game where a brave knight goes on a quest.

47
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:55,070
During this quest, the knight encounters treasure chests.

48
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:59,660
Now, each time the knight touches a chest, it opens up and tadaa.

49
00:02:59,690 --> 00:03:01,970
The coins inside it all jump out.

50
00:03:02,510 --> 00:03:03,560
Now here's the catch.

51
00:03:03,590 --> 00:03:09,230
Our game has not one, not two, but many treasure chests scattered in different levels.

52
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:13,310
We want every chest to do the same magical opening act.

53
00:03:13,730 --> 00:03:18,710
So how can we make this happen without writing the same code for every single chest?

54
00:03:18,950 --> 00:03:20,780
Here comes the function.

55
00:03:21,770 --> 00:03:28,640
We can write a function named Open Chest that contains the code to make the chest open and coins pop

56
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:29,030
out.

57
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:34,370
Now, every time the knight touches a chest, we simply call the open chest function.

58
00:03:34,610 --> 00:03:38,570
It's like telling the game, hey, do the chest opening thing now and voila!

59
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:41,540
The chest opens and coins come out and ta da!

60
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:45,110
And no matter where the chest is in the game, it works.

61
00:03:46,010 --> 00:03:51,920
And to give you a real world scratch example of functions in DOS, there is a popular project in scratch

62
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:56,600
called Smooth Movement Engine, and I encourage you to check it out for yourself.

63
00:03:57,050 --> 00:03:59,000
But clicking play on this project.

64
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:03,650
Notice how all these sprites move in a very fluid motion.

65
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:05,450
And if we click next.

66
00:04:06,470 --> 00:04:12,020
And next again you can see all the projects that utilize smooth movement.

67
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:18,410
Anyway, if we click this see inside button, you will see that there is this code block called Custom

68
00:04:18,410 --> 00:04:18,950
Block.

69
00:04:18,950 --> 00:04:23,330
And if we zoom out you will see all the different functions.

70
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:25,670
And yes, they're quite complex.

71
00:04:25,670 --> 00:04:27,890
So don't worry about trying to understand how it works.

72
00:04:27,890 --> 00:04:31,700
The important thing here is what the functions do.

73
00:04:31,820 --> 00:04:37,250
So if we move over to the My Blocks tab you'll see all these different blocks.

74
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:40,760
But basically these blocks are all used to call the functions.

75
00:04:40,760 --> 00:04:44,270
So let's say we had a sprite and we wanted it to move up.

76
00:04:44,270 --> 00:04:49,850
So let's first delete all these sprites so you can see this in action and also show the sprite.

77
00:04:50,470 --> 00:04:53,440
Now we want to ask where sprite to move forward 100 steps.

78
00:04:53,770 --> 00:04:58,090
Now, instead of using the movement code block, we can call this function here.

79
00:04:58,360 --> 00:05:01,420
So as you can see this block gives a lot of options.

80
00:05:01,420 --> 00:05:07,060
So let's say we wanted to move 100 steps in two seconds with an easing type of one.

81
00:05:07,870 --> 00:05:08,920
Clicking play.

82
00:05:08,920 --> 00:05:12,790
As you can see, our sprite moves smoothly down our screen.

83
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:19,510
So now let's say we wanted to turn and move forward again so we could grab this smooth turn block.

84
00:05:19,630 --> 00:05:25,930
So let's say we want to smooth turn 45 degrees in one second with a easing type of three.

85
00:05:26,290 --> 00:05:30,790
Then we can just duplicate this other smooth movement and stick it at the end.

86
00:05:31,210 --> 00:05:35,260
Now if we hit play, notice how our sprite moves slowly and smoothly forward.

87
00:05:36,670 --> 00:05:40,330
Then takes a smooth turn and finally moves forward again.

88
00:05:40,780 --> 00:05:43,240
And all I took was three blocks of code.

89
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:48,940
But if we were trying to manually create the smooth movement for each movement, it would take ages

90
00:05:48,940 --> 00:05:53,500
and our code would be so confusing to understand what was actually going on.

91
00:05:53,590 --> 00:05:59,410
So in summary, functions are one of the programmer's best tools to write efficient, organized, and

92
00:05:59,410 --> 00:06:00,460
clean code.

93
00:06:00,550 --> 00:06:07,600
In coding, a function is like a handy helper that does a specific job whenever we ask it, and it's

94
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:11,080
so reliable, we know it works because it's been tested and reused.

95
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:17,560
And so by mastering functions, you're well on your way to becoming a skilled coder who can make complex

96
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:19,510
and interactive programs.

